# TOEFL Reading Day59
## Topic: Volcanic Eruptions

Volcanic eruptions occur when materials from beneath the Earth's surface reach the atmosphere or the surface. Deep underground, intense heat can melt rock and form magma. When pressure builds, magma may move upward through cracks in the Earth's crust.

Once magma reaches the surface, it is called lava. An eruption may also release volcanic ash and gases into the atmosphere. These materials can affect the surrounding environment. Ash may cover vegetation and buildings, while gases can influence air quality.

Although volcanic eruptions can cause significant damage, volcanic activity can also create new land and contribute minerals to soil. Over long periods, volcanic regions may therefore support diverse ecosystems.

Scientists monitor volcanic activity by measuring factors such as earthquakes, ground deformation, and gas emissions. These observations can help them understand changes occurring beneath a volcano.

